#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171850 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172044 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb1745c5-6485-5a9d-9e8f-19492928e4b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Position Function of an Object Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/id #uuid "dd9a6e6a-e3d1-5684-8a14-7da410461aad" :question/math-academy-id "e-8642" :question/problem "A particle moves along a **clockwise** circular path with center $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $3 \\textrm{ rad/s}.$ Given that the object is at the position $(7,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the position function of the particle." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf {r}(t)= R\\left(\\cos \\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin \\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(7,0),$ we have that $\\mathbf r(0)=\\,7\\mathbf i,$ and we can conclude that the radius of the circle $R=7.$\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=7$ and $\\omega= -3  \\textrm{ rad/s}$ (negative because the motion is clockwise), we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =7(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3t)\\,j) \\\\  & =7(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171851 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172045 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d9865d-2e8e-59fe-9a81-8935df037303" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity and Speed of a Particle Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/id #uuid "cfcca273-3b96-5812-8b0d-029875f0585a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8643" :question/problem "An object moves along a circular path with position $\\mathbf {r}(t)= 5\\cos \\pi t \\, \\mathbf i + 5\\sin \\pi t \\,\\mathbf j$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-5\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,i+5\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, we calculate the magnitude of the velocity vector as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-5\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t)^{2}+(5\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{\\pi }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\pi t+25{\\pi }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\pi t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{\\pi }^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡\\pi t+{cos}^{2}⁡\\pi t)} \\\\  & =5\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the object's speed is $5 \\pi \\textrm{ m/s}.$\n\nNote that the speed is constant, but the velocity is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf v|| = R|\\omega|,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the radius of the circle, and $\\omega$ is the angular velocity.\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $R=5\\,\\textrm m$ and the angular frequency $\\omega = \\pi \\,\\textrm{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf v|| = 5\\cdot |\\pi| = 5\\pi \\textrm{ m/s}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171852 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172046 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "17787055-19de-5dca-b1f9-e97a3a7c2aff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration of a Particle Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/id #uuid "5614ccb1-e2be-5944-8229-61d62e5860fc" :question/math-academy-id "e-8644" :question/problem "An object moves along a circular path with position $\\mathbf {r}(t)= 4(\\cos 3t\\,  \\mathbf i + \\sin 3t\\, \\mathbf j)$ meters. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =(v(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-36\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i-36\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the magnitude of the acceleration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||a(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-36\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)^{2}+(-36\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{36}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡3t+{36}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡3t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{36}^{2}} \\\\  & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the acceleration is $36\\, \\textrm{m/s}^2.$\n\nNote that $||\\mathbf a||$ is constant, but $\\mathbf a$ is not.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a = -\\omega^2\\mathbf r \\qquad \\Rightarrow \\qquad ||\\mathbf a|| = \\omega^2||\\mathbf r||.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $R=4\\,\\textrm m$ and the angular frequency $\\omega = 3\\,\\textrm{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf a|| = 3^2\\cdot |4| = 36\\, \\textrm{m/s}^2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172653 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172045 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d9865d-2e8e-59fe-9a81-8935df037303" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity and Speed of a Particle Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8891fd30-f42f-558f-b5d9-fcd72fcf169d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2i + 2j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171544} {:answer/id #uuid "f01b1363-0b53-581c-81a3-98aed85e522c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i - 2j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171545} {:answer/id #uuid "282c14c4-3935-5d09-a461-e478579f54b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4i + 4j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171546} {:answer/id #uuid "4c17b8e3-297a-594a-94a4-04490bab6793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171547} {:answer/id #uuid "58648b11-825c-5047-86e7-3cf9a14e16aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8i - 8j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171548}] :answer-field/id #uuid "cb3ff474-1279-513d-8c8a-ac6df8e93849" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171543}] :question/id #uuid "8ffff090-3020-5818-a531-b90587e7f062" :question/math-academy-id "q-97566" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $4rad/s$. Given that the particle is located at $(2, 0)$ when $t = 0$, find the velocity of the particle at $t = \\frac{π}{4}$ seconds."}]}
